      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Published by the BMJ Publishing Group Limited. For permission to use (where not already granted under a licence) please go to http://www.bmj.com/company/products-services/rights-and-licensing/",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
            "content": "To assess the distribution of pressure and shear-related forces acting on atherosclerotic plaques and their association with lesion characteristics using coronary CT angiography (cCTA)-based computational fluid dynamics (CFD) model of epicardial coronary arteries."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "Patient-specific models of epicardial coronary arteries were reconstructed from cCTA in 80 patients (12 women, 63.8±9.0\u2005years). The pressure and wall shear stress (WSS) in left anterior descending coronary arteries were assessed using CFD. High-risk plaques were defined as the presence of at least one of the following adverse plaque characteristics: low-density plaque, positive remodelling, napkin-ring sign and spotty calcification."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "At resting condition, 39.5% of stenotic segments (% diameter stenosis 52.3±14.4%) were exposed to high WSS (>40\u2005dyne/cm(2)). When the stenotic lesion was subdivided into three segments, the distribution of WSS was different from that of pressure change and its magnitude was highest at minimal lumen area (p<0.001). High pressure gradient, proximal location, small lumen and short length were independent determinants of WSS (all p<0.05). The plaques exposed to the highest WSS tertile had a significantly greater proportion of high-risk plaques. The addition of WSS to % diameter stenosis significantly improved the measures of discrimination and reclassification of high-risk plaques (area under the curves from 0.540 to 0.718, p=0.031; net reclassification index 0.827, p<0.001)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "content": "The cCTA-based CFD method can improve the identification of high-risk plaques and the risk stratification for coronary artery disease patients by providing non-invasive measurements of WSS affecting coronary plaques."
          }
        ]
      },
